{# THE PLAIN-TEXT PART, AND IT IS WRITTEN FIRST ON PURPOSE.

   Mail clients are not browsers: a `multipart/alternative` message with no text part scores badly
   with every spam filter worth the name, and the text part is the version a filter can read without
   executing anything. This is deliverability work (PRD validation #3), not a courtesy — which is
   why it must say the *same* thing as the HTML part rather than being a stub.

   Whitespace control matters here in a way it does not in an HTML template: what is between the tags
   is literally what lands in the mailbox. `{%- ... -%}` and the file's exact line breaks are the
   layout. #}
